WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.170
When I was taking this script around, and -and because it's a love story between a mother and a daughter,

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.469
I remember every -every man I talked to who

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:10.888
was raised with sisters or, who had a daughter...

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:16.319
said "I know this. That's my wife and my daughter." or "That's my sister or my mom." And guys who didn't,

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.350
as they said "I don't... Do women fight like this?"

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.740
Like, ohh...

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:27.840
...you've never seen this, because why would you know that this is what this relationship is?

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.520
[Ripley]: This is Ripley, last survivor of the Nostromo...

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.240
...signing off.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.620
The movie "Alien" provides a fascinating case study

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.660
into how women are typically written.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.440
In an early draft of the script, the screenwriter wrote that the crew is unisex and all parts are interchangeable

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:54.000
for men or women. The character of Ripley can't really reinforce stereotypes

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.740
about women, because the writer did not have a woman in mind in the first place.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.900
In many ways, a largely genderless hero

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.800
is a great character to have in a critically acclaimed, Hollywood blockbuster.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.660
There's something cathartic about not having to think about gender in a main character.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.740
Writing without a woman in mind means a writer can

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.430
explore universal human experiences, without worrying about falling into stereotype.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:19.500
It's definitely fun and exciting to see a character who doesn't get treated differently because she's a woman.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.140
She is a well-written character with qualities not associated with any particular gender.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.540
Ripley in "Alien" is a strong woman character,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.520
because gender plays a very little role, and her strengths could just as easily exist in a masculine character.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.240
Ripley is just one of the many valid ways to approach writing women.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.009
She plays out the fantasy that gender can be ignored and that leads to a great movie with a great lead...

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:45.680
...but a big strength of film is its ability to tell different human experiences,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.250
and the real world is usually much different than Ripley's.  Your gender plays a huge part in your identity.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.119
There's nothing wrong with a movie that explores a character without exploring their gender,

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:59.218
but a movie that incorporates gender into the story can lead to moving and diverse character arcs.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.060
[Hannibal Lector]: Good evening, Clarice.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.000
Maybe the best example of writing women with gender at the forefront is Clarice Starling in "Silence of the Lambs."

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:12.700
She stands out in the masculine dominated world of the FBI, and she is rarely taken seriously by her masculine

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.340
counterparts. Most guys either subtly check her out or blatantly hit on her.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:20.269
Frederic Chilton thinks she was only assigned to interview Lector because she's a pretty girl.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.520
[Starling]: I graduated from UVA doctor, it is not a Charm School.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.840
[Professor]: Good, then you should be able to remember the rules.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:31.159
Perhaps the moments that emphasize her gender the most are the subjective point of view shots of men gazing at her.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.799
They are always looking either in contempt or attraction,

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.700
clearly marking her as an outsider and, there's more to pointing out her gender than just to show sexism in the workplace.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.800
Her gender has clearly shaped parts of her character arc.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:50.080
She personally identifies with the women abducted by Buffalo Bill.  Seeing the wake of one of the girls gives her a flashback of her father's death.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.520
The opening shot of the movie has Clarice pulling herself up a hill, and her goal in the movie is

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:58.190
to save the captured girls from holes they cannot pull themselves out of.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.040
[Lector]: What did you do?

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.540
[Starling, whispering]: I went... downstairs...

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.300
...I crept up into the barn.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.080
I was so scared to look inside, but I had to.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.360
[Lector]: What did you see Clarice, what did you see?

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.560
[Starling]: Lambs.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.760
[Whispering]: They were screaming.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.840
[Woman yelling]: Just get me out of here!
*Dog barking*

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.460
[Lector]: You ran away?

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.760
*Dog whimpering*

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.760
[Starling]: No.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:27.680
[Starling]: Catherine...

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.260
*Dog still whimpering*

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.180
...I'm gonna get you out of there, but right now you listen to me.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.680
The climactic finale of the movie is the ultimate visual link between the Buffalo Bill

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.270
abductions, and the men looking down on Clarice. In Buffalo Bill's lair,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.980
he watches over her while she's unable to see him.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.240
[Lector]: Don't you feel eyes moving over your body, Clarice?

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.500
Of course, this scene is creepy and

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:52.250
incredible all on its own, but it also means more because she is a woman and because we have already inhabited her

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.029
perspective, as a woman in a world where men glare at her and objectify her.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.140
She has a universal journey of triumph against adversity,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:04.180
but the obstacles in her path are shaped by the fact that she is a woman. If she were played by a man, the movie

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.420
would feel drastically different. Unlike Ripley an "Alien," Starling's gender elevates

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.920
the emotional payoff of the movie. Starling's gender is a key component of her character arc.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:19.309
It's a realistic, grounded and important story, but a movie starring a woman doesn't always have to focus on the struggles of being a woman.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.690
The bride in "Kill Bill" is certainly not genderless. Her story begins when she's shot at her own wedding while pregnant. Immediately

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:37.199
after waking from a coma, she goes on her quest for revenge on her assailant, her former lover Bill. The bride embodies the character

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:44.100
traits of a traditional masculine hero. Revenge is a classic staple of drama and a common plot line for narratives typically starring men.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:50.480
In contrast, "Alien" and "Silence of the Lambs" have more in common with a survival narrative. Ripley survives an invasion of an alien monster...

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.910
...Starling does choose to confront the villain herself,. but much of her story involves surviving events that are out of her control.

00:04:56.919 --> 00:04:59.339
She has to withstand the gaze of other men,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.839
especially in the climax when she's blind to the villain staring right at her. With "Kill Bill,"

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.859
the story is completely driven by the hero. Her journey is not a situation

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.289
she's thrust into, but rather a conscious plan

00:05:10.289 --> 00:05:12.659
she devised for herself. This quest for revenge

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:20.309
echoes the popular storylines of old American westerns, where heroes decide to reconcile some past trauma. Like Ripley in Alien,

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.140
she fulfills a fantasy of transcending her gender...

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:28.649
...but instead of being genderless like Ripley, the bride fulfills the fantasy of being the action hero archetype,

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:36.030
traditionally associated with a man. The bride does not asked gender-neutral; she acts in a masculine way, just like the male heroes before her.

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:40.739
She's aggressive; She takes charge of the story and she gets her revenge. "Kill Bill" fulfills

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:46.409
the fantasy that a female character can act like a male hero. The movie gives a fresh perspective on writing women.

00:05:46.409 --> 00:05:49.079
She is never a victim in this movie. When men wrong her,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.130
she always fights back immediately. Starling had to tolerate the men in her life,

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:56.999
but the bride doesn't have to. Like the characters before her, she's tapping into the

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:04.500
universal fantasy of being able to right every wrong. The trade-off is that this movie does not seem to tap into a distinctly feminine experience.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.120
What I mean by that is, her near-death feels like this symbolic death of a delicate, more feminine side of her.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:15.540
As appropriate and badass as this transformation might be, there does seem to be a part of the female experience

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.609
missing in this story. All three of these movies write women with some element of a masculine

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:24.209
perspective. "Alien" is a movie where the characters could have been played by men.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:30.479
"The Silence of the Lambs" has Clarice interacting almost exclusively with men, and "Kill Bill" has a woman playing a masculine role.

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:33.999
It's also worth noting that all of these movies were written and directed by men.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.539
So, it makes sense that there are some masculine elements in each of them.

00:06:37.539 --> 00:06:42.039
Maybe the last piece of the puzzle is a movie that embraces an experience exclusive to women.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.240
[Marion]: You're not even worth state tuition Christine.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.540
[Ladybird]: My name is Lady Bird!

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:46.060
[Marion]: *stammers* Well,

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:48.060
actually, it's not. And, it's ridiculous...

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.539
Unlike most coming-of-age movies Christine's romantic interests in "Lady Bird" are fleeting and unimportant.

00:06:52.539 --> 00:06:57.939
She paints over her crushes' names before moving out... a visual reminder that she has completely moved on. And, those

00:06:58.009 --> 00:07:02.919
relationships do not have a permanent effect on her. The relationship with her mother is explored with much more detail.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:09.819
The opening shot instantly shows us the movie's emotional core. In the script, Greta Gerwig describes this opening shot as "a modern-day

00:07:09.819 --> 00:07:12.819
romantic Mary Cassatt ripoff painting of motherhood."

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:19.720
Mary Cassatt was an impressionist painter known for her images of women in social and private life. Unlike many painters at that time, Cassat

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:25.659
consistently portrayed her women with a dignity that suggested deeper, more intimate views of her characters. People

00:07:25.659 --> 00:07:30.429
look at Cassatt's paintings and relate to the mother-child interactions in their familiar activities.

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:35.739
The thoughtful expressions of many of her subjects gives them a certain intellectual look, like there's more than meets the eye.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:40.960
I think a Greta Gerwig wants to capture that exact spirit in this movie. "Lady Bird" gives an incredible

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.229
look at the complicated relationship

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:47.619
between two women. Who would have thought a mother's opinion on the clothes her daughter tries on could have so many layers?

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.620
[Marion]: I want you to be...

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:53.000
...the very best version of yourself, that you can be.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:56.160
[Ladybird]: What if this is the best version?

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:02.780
This scene ends with the mom trying to think of what to say, and cutting to the next scene before she's able to. Gerwig noted

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.940
that Lady Bird and her mother are most intimate when they are unconscious in the first frame.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.719
We will never see them be this close to each other for the rest of the film. Ironically,

00:08:10.719 --> 00:08:12.879
they also look extremely similar, which Gerwig

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:19.059
reinforces in the match cuts of them driving. Despite their similarities, these characters try to be as different from each other as possible.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:23.540
It's this complicated back-and-forth relationship that makes their scenes so full of depth.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.860
[Ladybird]: You are so infuriating!

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.560
[Marion]: Please stop yelling.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:26.680
[Ladybird]: I'm not yelling.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:28.600
[Ladybird]: *Gasps* Ohh, thats perfect.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:29.440
[Marion]: Do you love it?

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.720
The characters are imperfect, and most importantly vulnerable. While there's value in the female badasses like Kill Bill,

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:41.950
there's also a strength in embracing the weaknesses and faults a person can have. It's about covering the entire spectrum of human experience,

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:48.460
where a female can act a masculine in one movie and feminine in another. Women directors and writers can give an honest and relatable depiction

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:51.429
of women experiences in addition to the fantasies of FBI

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:56.770
Agents, space explorers, and assassins. and it's important that all these kinds of characters exist for women.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.799
Each of these characters gives us a different look into the human experience

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:03.489
Maybe a well-written character played by a woman challenges you to think a little differently.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.620
Maybe they challenge you to look without gender or to see women from a new perspective. If it works, it works.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.720
There's no wrong way to write women.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:15.790
At the end of the day, the great part about cinema is how it gives us a window into worlds

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:20.529
we wouldn't have seen on our own. Combine that with the connectivity of the Internet, and the possibilities are endless.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:24.340
If you're looking to expand your understanding across many different subjects,

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:28.299
then I highly recommend the award-winning documentaries on CuriosityStream.

